      "note_id": "borrowed_from_ukrainian",
      "kind": "helpful_transfer",
      "text": "English and Ukrainian are distant cousins, so look-alike words are rare. The first words here are English words that came from Ukrainian, mostly food, music and money; a few came by way of another language, like Yiddish for borscht or French and Polish for Cossack. The last ones are old shared words (brother, sister, mother, two, three, night, nose, water, wolf, widow): true relatives from a common ancestor, not borrowings, and thousands of years have changed how they sound.",
